00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:30,820
Everybody's investment will be at least partly repaid,

238
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:33,240
because everybody gets a piece of the action.

239
00:17:35,600 --> 00:17:40,020
Seven designs for the Air Force competition were submitted for final evaluation.

240
00:17:40,021 --> 00:17:47,700
All of the seven contractors came in with designs that were very feasible and that could have been built.

241
00:17:48,220 --> 00:17:53,780
The question was, which were the best and then how did we determine that they really were good enough?

242
00:17:56,540 --> 00:18:00,440
Two manufacturers with strong experience in stealth technology.

243
00:18:01,260 --> 00:18:03,080
Northrop Grumman with its B-2.

244
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:08,420
And Lockheed Martin with its F-117 led the way.

245
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:19,880
Northrop's Advanced Tactical Fighter, or ATF design, was for an alien-looking aircraft with diamond platform wings and huge V-tails.

246
00:18:19,881 --> 00:18:24,040
It was a design that stressed speed and stealth.

247
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:33,040
Northrop came in with an airplane that really, from the very beginning, looked just like the YF-23 that eventually was built and flown.

248
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:37,400
Lockheed's entry echoed that of the F-117.

249
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:45,640
Its vectored thrust, arrowhead shape, trapezoidal wings, and four tails ensured that the aircraft would be maneuverable.

250
00:18:47,240 --> 00:18:57,020
The fact was that Lockheed and Northrop had significant advantages in the fact that they had built a stealth aircraft and flown them.

251
00:18:57,460 --> 00:18:59,060
It was a big credibility factor there.

252
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:07,280
On October 31, 1986, both Lockheed and Northrop's ATF designs were declared the winners.

253
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:13,700
Under the terms of the DemVal competition, each team would build two aircraft.

254
00:19:16,700 --> 00:19:22,660
At the end of the process, one of two designs would become America's new advanced tactical fighter.

255
00:19:24,380 --> 00:19:30,620
Costing billions of dollars, the new fighter would make a technological leap into the 21st century.

256
00:19:30,621 --> 00:19:41,380
The F-A-22's radar system gives the aircraft a first look, first shot, first kill capability.

257
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:48,640
That means it can see an enemy plane first, fire a missile, and destroy the target without the pilot ever knowing.

258
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:51,420
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

259
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:55,520
They were never trained for this.

260
00:19:55,820 --> 00:19:56,840
You don't get scared.

261
00:19:56,841 --> 00:20:01,540
The Soviet Union, the shapes of the two rival designs were finally unveiled.

262
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:10,021
And now, ladies and gentlemen, I proudly present to you the YF-22A prototype for U.S.

263
00:20:10,022 --> 00:20:12,740
air superiority in the 21st century.

264
00:20:12,741 --> 00:20:26,741
On behalf of the entire team, I am honored to present the YF-23.

265
00:20:36,220 --> 00:20:41,400
Northrop's version, called the YF-23, closely resembled its original design.

266
00:20:42,740 --> 00:20:46,799
Well, it was a most unusual looking aircraft, very futuristic.

267
00:20:46,800 --> 00:20:53,282
It had twin V-tails as opposed to what was called a cruciform, twin verticals and twin horizontals.

268
00:20:53,283 --> 00:20:59,960
It had a large trapezoidal wing, and it had a very slender shape when looked at from the edge on view.

269
00:20:59,961 --> 00:21:12,519
In contrast, Lockheed's design, called the YF-22, seemed surprisingly conventional, with four tail surfaces, vectored thrust, a broad, solid body, and a conventional

270
00:21:12,520 --> 00:21:13,220
wing.

271
00:21:13,840 --> 00:21:25,498
But unlike Lockheed's other stealth aircraft, the F-117, radar-absorbent materials were not applied over the whole of the F-22, but used selectively on its edges,

272
00:21:25,499 --> 00:21:28,520
cavities, and other crucial surface areas.

273
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:41,360
You walk around the airplane, everywhere you look, what you see is something that's designed to do the job in the most efficient and effective way.

274
00:21:41,361 --> 00:21:46,500
And no wasted space, no wasted capability.

275
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:52,340
It's truly an airplane that's intended and has been optimized for its job.

276
00:21:55,600 --> 00:21:58,860
The F-22 carries its weapons internally.

277
00:21:58,861 --> 00:22:04,740
four weapons bays are hidden in the central mid-body section. Six missiles

278
00:22:04,741 --> 00:22:09,920
can be carried in the ventral bays which are covered with bifold doors. The side

279
00:22:09,921 --> 00:22:15,260
bays will each hold one sidewinder missile carried on a trapeze launcher.

280
00:22:15,261 --> 00:22:20,340
The mid-body section also houses the fighters landing gear and complex inlet

281
00:22:20,341 --> 00:22:28,420
ducts. Right from day one on the F-22 we decided to put S-shaped

282
00:22:28,421 --> 00:22:33,400
inlet ducts on it so the airplane is built with s-shaped inlet ducts so that

283
00:22:33,401 --> 00:22:40,800
there's no way a radar is ever seeing the forward face of the jet engine

284
00:22:40,801 --> 00:22:45,260
attached to the mid-body is the forebody which accommodates the cockpit and

285
00:22:45,261 --> 00:22:52,200
advanced avionics both the YF-23 and the YF-22 are impressive looking

286
00:22:52,201 --> 00:22:59,160
machines but their performance still needs to be tested the most crucial

287
00:22:59,161 --> 00:23:03,120
stage of the competition is still to come the flight testing

288
00:23:03,121 --> 00:23:17,121
Northrop was first in the air in August 1990 the YF-23 flown by Paul Metz went airborne

289
00:23:40,949 --> 00:23:43,160
The test was a huge success.

290
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:49,200
But Lockheed was quick to respond.

291
00:23:49,201 --> 00:23:53,280
and on September the 29th at Edwards Air Force Base in California,

292
00:23:53,800 --> 00:23:57,620
Chief Test Pilot Dave Ferguson prepared the Raptor for its maiden flight.

293
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:11,520
And I thought, the only thing in this airplane that's ever flown before is me.

294
00:24:11,880 --> 00:24:15,220
And I think in the back of my mind I was saying, please fly, please fly.

295
00:24:15,340 --> 00:24:17,500
And I pulled the nose up and it just lifted off.

296
00:24:40,143 --> 00:24:46,040
I was fully aware we had a wonderful flying airplane and the handling qualities in the

297
00:24:46,041 --> 00:24:50,180
takeoff and landing, or power approach as we call it, were just absolutely superb.

298
00:24:54,500 --> 00:24:59,040
When I landed and Sherm met me at the airplane, I said, hey boss, we really have a winner here.

299
00:25:00,860 --> 00:25:06,220
The way the F-22 performed was no surprise to anybody who was involved in the program,

300
00:25:06,360 --> 00:25:07,368
not at all.

301
00:25:07,369 --> 00:25:15,520
I mean, my money has been on the F-22 from early 1985, and it'll be there until I'm gone.

302
00:25:16,040 --> 00:25:22,240
Over the next three months, the Raptor underwent a whole series of tests.

303
00:25:22,760 --> 00:25:27,260
The Air Force required both teams to give them performance projections,

304
00:25:28,080 --> 00:25:32,040
and then they were going to actually compare that with what the airplanes actually did in flight,

305
00:25:32,180 --> 00:25:35,080
subsonic, supersonic, at different altitudes, and so forth.

306
00:25:36,160 --> 00:25:40,520
The winner of this stage would earn a contract for 650 aircraft.

307
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:45,140
The decision would hinge not just on what the contractors promised,

308
00:25:45,141 --> 00:25:48,560
but on the Air Force's confidence in their ability to deliver.

309
00:25:50,420 --> 00:25:54,900
We expected to get a lot of flying done in the 90 days.

310
00:25:54,980 --> 00:25:59,040
We actually got 72 flights out of two airplanes in 90 days.

311
00:26:02,200 --> 00:26:04,620
And that is about as good as you can do.

312
00:26:06,200 --> 00:26:10,340
During flight testing, the Raptor had beaten Northrop's YF-23

313
00:26:10,341 --> 00:26:13,060
in a number of crucial performance areas.

314
00:26:15,140 --> 00:26:21,220
We'd focused on the supersonic testing, including super cruising,

315
00:26:21,580 --> 00:26:24,140
and we did something that Northrop didn't do,

316
00:26:24,200 --> 00:26:25,920
and that is we did launch a couple of missiles.

317
00:26:25,921 --> 00:26:31,920
We launched a sidewinder out of the internal side bay on our prototype airplanes,

318
00:26:32,060 --> 00:26:35,620
and we launched an AMRAAM, long-range air-to-air missile,

319
00:26:35,740 --> 00:26:37,580
out of the internal weapon bays.

320
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:43,280
The YF-22 had clearly shown that in every category,

321
00:26:43,281 --> 00:26:46,180
it was far superior to any existing fighter.

322
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:52,460
The Air Force was very impressed by what Lockheed had done.

323
00:26:52,560 --> 00:26:54,580
Their flight test program was very aggressive.

324
00:26:55,380 --> 00:26:57,780
They flew hard and fast.

325
00:26:58,080 --> 00:27:01,240
They flew many more hours in sorties than Northrop did.

326
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:03,480
And all of that gave the Air Force confidence

327
00:27:03,481 --> 00:27:05,880
that they knew what they were doing, high confidence,

328
00:27:06,100 --> 00:27:07,900
and they could build a superior airplane.

329
00:27:07,901 --> 00:27:17,440
but it would be events in 1991 that would carve out the raptors future

330
00:27:17,441 --> 00:27:27,020
22 minutes after midnight on january 17th 1991

331
00:27:27,021 --> 00:27:33,560
lockheed's stealth f-117 spearheaded u.s strikes against saddam hussein's regime

332
00:27:33,561 --> 00:27:40,360
The performance of Lockheed's stealth bombers during Operation Desert Storm

333
00:27:40,361 --> 00:27:44,100
gave the company and its aircraft some priceless publicity.

334
00:27:45,760 --> 00:27:52,820
I think clearly Lockheed was benefited in 1991 by the Gulf War,

335
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:55,640
where the F-117 was a star performer.

336
00:27:55,960 --> 00:28:00,800
And there were skeptics of stealth, even as late as that.

337
00:28:00,801 --> 00:28:04,800
that had to be beneficial to the program.

338
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:11,500
But another aircraft also emerged from the Gulf War with a glowing reputation.

339
00:28:18,060 --> 00:28:22,880
The F-15, the aircraft destined to be replaced by the ATF,

340
00:28:23,220 --> 00:28:28,260
emphatically confirmed its status as the foremost air superiority fighter in the world.

341
00:28:28,261 --> 00:28:36,460
now it appeared that the need for an advanced stealthy fighter the f-22 might be totally

342
00:28:36,461 --> 00:28:42,460
unfounded see it's not like the f-15 wasn't any good it's a good aircraft it's still a good

343
00:28:42,461 --> 00:28:48,600
aircraft it will be a good aircraft for years to come you could even just tool up the factory and

344
00:28:48,601 --> 00:28:54,220
keep building new f-15s why not hey i'm gonna bring him up now but not everyone agrees the big

345
00:28:54,221 --> 00:29:00,180
weakness in their argument is they are making a statement about world conditions today and what

346
00:29:00,181 --> 00:29:07,070
the threats are today. The real issue is what capability you want for 2025 or 2030.

347
00:29:07,071 --> 00:29:07,900
The people

348
00:29:07,901 --> 00:29:12,480
who say you don't need this, they think the world is not going to change in the next 20 years.

349
00:29:13,400 --> 00:29:21,200
And that is a hell of an assertion. By April 1991, bogged down by the F-15 debate,

350
00:29:21,201 --> 00:29:21,901
the U.S.

351
00:29:21,902 --> 00:29:25,960
Air Force prepared to announce the winner of the Advanced Tactical Fighter contract.

352
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:31,700
But would the Raptor emerge from the controversy unscathed?

353
00:29:36,420 --> 00:29:41,700
The F-A-22 is the first fighter aircraft with the ability to supercruise.

354
00:29:42,160 --> 00:29:47,220
That means it can fly at a velocity of one and a half times the speed of sound or greater

355
00:29:47,221 --> 00:29:49,760
without needing to engage afterburners.

356
00:29:49,761 --> 00:29:52,480
modern marvels will return in a moment.

357
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:02,000
Lockheed announced that it intended to locate the F-22's headquarters in Georgia,

358
00:30:02,520 --> 00:30:04,920
where the Raptors' forward fuselage would be built.

359
00:30:06,440 --> 00:30:11,100
General Dynamics would build the F-22's mid-body section in Fort Worth, Texas,

360
00:30:11,540 --> 00:30:15,540
and Boeing would manufacture the wings and tail in Seattle, Washington.

361
00:30:19,760 --> 00:30:26,020
But just eight months after the contract was awarded, the program hit its first major snag.

362
00:30:26,021 --> 00:30:28,980
We lost one of the YF-22s.

363
00:30:29,520 --> 00:30:34,400
Fortunately, the test pilot, who's a good friend of mine then and now, walked away unharmed.

364
00:30:36,300 --> 00:30:39,660
During preliminary testing, the unthinkable happened.

365
00:30:40,180 --> 00:30:44,840
A YF-22 flown by Tom Morkenfeld crashed just after takeoff.

366
00:30:44,841 --> 00:30:55,640
as this unique footage shows the aircraft's thrust vectoring system forces it to belly land on the

367
00:30:55,641 --> 00:31:03,980
runway a key element of the Raptors design thrust vectoring uses movable exhaust nozzles

368
00:31:03,981 --> 00:31:07,460
to alter the angle of thrust from the two Pratt and Whitney engines

369
00:31:07,461 --> 00:31:15,420
as the raptor makes its low-level flyby tom morgen felt keeps the stick forward to keep

370
00:31:15,421 --> 00:31:21,800
the nose down but as the landing gear is retracted the thrust vectoring engages and

371
00:31:21,801 --> 00:31:27,180
pushes the aircraft towards the tarmac as the pilot struggles to correct this change in direction

372
00:31:27,181 --> 00:31:36,980
the raptor seesaws the fundamental error there had nothing to do with the airplane

373
00:31:36,981 --> 00:31:44,320
It had to do with the fact that you don't fly a green airplane and fly it at low speeds, at low altitudes.

374
00:31:44,820 --> 00:31:48,220
The cause of that accident was stupidity on the part of the management.

375
00:31:48,360 --> 00:31:50,000
It had nothing to do with technology.

376
00:31:51,520 --> 00:31:56,360
Despite the loss of the stealth aircraft, the program had achieved its major goals.

377
00:31:56,361 --> 00:32:08,273
10 million man-hours of analysis, 4,000 hours of radar testing, and hundreds of hours of flight testing had gone into the development of the aircraft, even before

378
00:32:08,274 --> 00:32:10,920
construction was given the go-ahead.

379
00:32:14,060 --> 00:32:20,940
In fact, the F-22 accomplished more flight testing than any other fighter prior to full-scale production.

380
00:32:20,941 --> 00:32:24,237
The first F-A-22 built for the U.S.

381
00:32:24,238 --> 00:32:33,940
Air Force was unveiled in a ceremony on April 9, 1997, at Lockheed's headquarters in Marietta, Georgia.

382
00:32:39,520 --> 00:32:44,800
Now Air Force pilots would get the opportunity to check out the new aircraft for themselves.

383
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:50,460
I would call the Raptor the Miss America of all aircraft. It's got the talent.

384
00:32:50,461 --> 00:32:54,660
It's got the bikini contest won. It's beautiful. It's got all the capabilities.

385
00:32:55,100 --> 00:32:56,920
It really wins the show in every aspect.

386
00:32:58,280 --> 00:32:59,980
The airplane is eye-watering.

387
00:33:00,520 --> 00:33:06,360
It does everything the pilot asks of it, and it is very good at what it does.

388
00:33:07,680 --> 00:33:10,280
First flown by the Air Force in 1997,

389
00:33:10,980 --> 00:33:17,200
pilots at Edwards Air Force Base have exceeded 2,000 flight test hours in more than 900 missions.

390
00:33:20,460 --> 00:33:28,360
The first time I went out, one F-822, me, against four F-16s.

391
00:33:30,500 --> 00:33:32,180
And they told me what they were going to do.

392
00:33:32,280 --> 00:33:35,520
They were going to do everything possible to defeat my systems.

393
00:33:37,200 --> 00:33:40,700
And I watched exactly what they did the entire time and shot them all.

394
00:33:43,920 --> 00:33:46,820
It was almost too easy, and I was almost laughing in the cockpit.

395
00:33:50,460 --> 00:33:56,660
A key breakthrough in the Raptor's design, its advanced cockpit and integrated avionics systems.

396
00:33:59,540 --> 00:34:05,940
But I think really where the Raptor gets its amazing capability is the fusion of all of the different sensors on the aircraft.

397
00:34:06,280 --> 00:34:12,780
You have a tactical scope that combines the information of all the other sensors on the aircraft into one display for the pilot.

398
00:34:13,220 --> 00:34:18,820
So as a pilot, you don't have to sort through the radar or another sensor to see what's going on around you.

399
00:34:18,821 --> 00:34:24,580
Information is power, and the way this airplane displays information to you, it gives you knowledge of the battle space.

400
00:34:25,180 --> 00:34:32,000
It's all about seeing what's out there in front of you and being able to make decisions about what to engage, when to engage, and how to engage it.

401
00:34:32,860 --> 00:34:36,860
I'd say integrated avionics does two things for me.

402
00:34:37,100 --> 00:34:38,700
Number one, it makes me a lot safer.

403
00:34:39,360 --> 00:34:41,660
It gives me less chance to crash my jet.

404
00:34:42,180 --> 00:34:45,460
It also makes every pilot who flies this aircraft more deadly.

405
00:34:45,461 --> 00:34:51,100
instead of having to do six or eight steps to achieve a kill you really only

406
00:34:51,101 --> 00:34:59,580
have to do one the Raptor carries a formidable array of ordnance

407
00:35:00,780 --> 00:35:06,160
all of the Raptors weapons are housed inside the aircraft two bays at the

408
00:35:06,161 --> 00:35:10,360
bottom of the plane use a pneumatic style hydraulic launcher that literally

409
00:35:10,361 --> 00:35:15,360
punch the missiles or JDAMs out of the aircraft with a force of 40 G's.

410
00:35:19,360 --> 00:35:22,360
And two side bays house air-to-air missiles.

411
00:35:22,361 --> 00:35:27,360
Here a trapeze launcher moves the missile outside the airframe very rapidly,

412
00:35:27,361 --> 00:35:30,360
a fraction of a second before the missile is fired.

413
00:35:31,360 --> 00:35:36,360
And to complement the Raptor's armament of eight missiles, the fighter also has a gun.

414
00:35:36,361 --> 00:35:41,120
At one point in the evolution of the Advanced Tactical Fighter program,

415
00:35:41,600 --> 00:35:45,980
the U.S. Air Force had raised the question of eliminating the gun to save weight.

416
00:35:48,560 --> 00:35:53,240
I think that the designers of the F-A-22 realized mistakes of the past,

417
00:35:53,320 --> 00:35:55,840
like the F-4 initially being designed without a gun,

418
00:35:56,140 --> 00:36:00,420
and realized that you should never say never about a threat that you're going to face

419
00:36:00,421 --> 00:36:02,320
or a situation you're going to find yourself in.

420
00:36:02,760 --> 00:36:04,860
And so the Raptor has designed itself a gun.

421
00:36:04,861 --> 00:36:07,300
Will anybody get close enough for us to use the gun?

422
00:36:07,380 --> 00:36:08,800
Well, hopefully not. Probably not.

423
00:36:10,340 --> 00:36:14,120
By the late 90s, pilots in the Raptor program were convinced

424
00:36:14,121 --> 00:36:16,820
that their aircraft was made of the right stuff

425
00:36:16,821 --> 00:36:22,560
and would easily be able to outperform and destroy any other fighter in existence.

426
00:36:23,340 --> 00:36:26,960
It is the sum of the parts that makes the F-A-22 so capable.

427
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:30,440
Probably most important in my eyes is the stealth.

428
00:36:30,441 --> 00:36:37,520
Having an aircraft that nobody can see is just a tremendous, tremendous advantage.

429
00:36:38,040 --> 00:36:44,500
The speed, the maneuverability, the precision, all those factors are incredible also.

430
00:36:45,120 --> 00:36:48,300
So when you put everything together, the Raptor's just incredible.

431
00:36:49,940 --> 00:36:55,680
But this belief was exclusively based on controlled flight and missile firing exercises.

432
00:36:55,681 --> 00:37:00,720
What the Raptors pilots really needed was combat experience.

433
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:03,180
And they were about to get it.

434
00:37:05,500 --> 00:37:08,740
America's untested fifth-generation stealth fighter

435
00:37:08,741 --> 00:37:13,640
was about to go head-to-head with a combat-seasoned air superiority fighter,

436
00:37:14,640 --> 00:37:16,420
the F-15 Eagle.

437
00:37:19,300 --> 00:37:24,360
The Pentagon recently estimated that the total development and production cost

438
00:37:24,361 --> 00:37:29,640
of its current plan for 279 Raptors would come to nearly $72 billion.

439
00:37:30,420 --> 00:37:32,860
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

440
00:37:35,260 --> 00:37:37,360
Final 2. Modern Marvels.

441
00:37:41,440 --> 00:37:45,997
Before the F-A-22 Raptor enters operational service with the U.S.

442
00:37:45,998 --> 00:37:48,100
Air Force in the fall of 2005,

443
00:37:48,660 --> 00:37:52,100
it will have completed thousands of hours of vigorous combat testing.

444
00:37:54,360 --> 00:38:02,820
But since Desert Storm, critics of the F-22 program claim that the F-15 Eagle, destined to be replaced by the Raptor,

445
00:38:03,180 --> 00:38:10,280
already has the attributes necessary to remain the world's preeminent air superiority fighter well into the new millennium.

446
00:38:18,080 --> 00:38:21,020
It is a view dismissed by the U.S. Air Force.

447
00:38:21,021 --> 00:38:35,020
If you look at the F-15 and the F-18 and compare it with existing fighters that are sold around the world today, you'll find that today we're almost at parity.

448
00:38:35,620 --> 00:38:47,080
If we ever run up against an enemy that has the ability in terms of the air crew to use those enemy fighters, we will have a tough time with the current generation.

449
00:38:47,081 --> 00:38:53,140
The F-15 is a great aircraft, and in air-to-air it is outstanding.

450
00:38:53,300 --> 00:38:57,180
However, with the production of new fighters that are being produced today

451
00:38:57,181 --> 00:39:00,160
and also some service-to-air threats,

452
00:39:00,640 --> 00:39:03,240
there are situations in the F-15 that would make me nervous.

453
00:39:09,380 --> 00:39:14,540
In March 2003, supporters of the F-15 got the opportunity to see

454
00:39:14,541 --> 00:39:18,000
whether or not the Eagle was still the best fighter in the sky.

455
00:39:24,680 --> 00:39:28,420
Five F-15s would go head-to-head with a single Raptor.

456
00:39:31,560 --> 00:39:34,320
Although no missiles would be used during the exercise,

457
00:39:34,321 --> 00:39:37,360
the soldiers would closely resemble actual combat.

458
00:39:38,780 --> 00:39:40,800
No mercy would be given by either side.

459
00:39:41,720 --> 00:39:44,720
This was a kill-or-be-killed exercise.

460
00:39:45,960 --> 00:39:48,520
There were five adversaries and me,

461
00:39:49,080 --> 00:39:53,320
and my biggest concern was running out of weapons too soon.

462
00:39:54,300 --> 00:39:58,220
All five F-15s were flown by experienced F-22 pilots.

463
00:39:59,160 --> 00:40:01,780
One by one, the Raptor brought them down.

464
00:40:03,720 --> 00:40:05,300
I could never see them.

465
00:40:05,700 --> 00:40:07,960
I never knew that they were there, and I died.

466
00:40:07,961 --> 00:40:09,360
You got something locked?

467
00:40:09,361 --> 00:40:16,820
I could hear him on the radio calling his simulated missile shots, Fox 2,

468
00:40:17,580 --> 00:40:21,820
and knowing that this was getting really unnerving

469
00:40:21,821 --> 00:40:25,440
because I could also tell his range was closing rapidly on me.

470
00:40:26,660 --> 00:40:35,020
I don't think anybody ever saw me the entire time while we were out in the airspace.

471
00:40:35,600 --> 00:40:37,700
Bandit, bandit. Fox.

472
00:40:37,701 --> 00:40:41,160
I could not find him no matter what I did,

473
00:40:41,340 --> 00:40:44,000
and the next time was when he flew directly over the top of my airplane.

474
00:40:44,220 --> 00:40:44,920
I saw him visually.

475
00:40:45,760 --> 00:40:48,400
In three, two, one, snap.

476
00:40:48,880 --> 00:40:51,580
I know firsthand from flying the Raptor against other aircraft

477
00:40:51,581 --> 00:40:53,440
and flying other aircraft against the Raptor

478
00:40:53,441 --> 00:40:55,940
that it's like clubbing baby seals.

479
00:40:56,080 --> 00:40:56,920
It's so easy.

480
00:40:58,500 --> 00:41:04,200
The first indication you have that an F-22 is out in your area of responsibility

481
00:41:04,201 --> 00:41:07,420
is when you are in your parachute heading down towards Earth

482
00:41:07,421 --> 00:41:09,780
and your jet is falling a little bit faster than you are.

483
00:41:12,480 --> 00:41:15,100
In combat testing with F-15s,

484
00:41:15,320 --> 00:41:19,060
the F-822 Raptor had emphatically proven its doubters wrong.

485
00:41:21,920 --> 00:41:26,440
There have been times when the Raptor has gone up two versus eight against F-15s,

486
00:41:26,560 --> 00:41:30,100
and it ends up being boring for the adversaries

487
00:41:30,101 --> 00:41:32,200
because no matter what they do, they die.

488
00:41:33,460 --> 00:41:36,020
Having flown the F-15 and now having flown the Raptor

489
00:41:36,021 --> 00:41:41,460
and seeing combat, I would not want to be on the receiving end of what the F-822 is capable of.

490
00:41:42,240 --> 00:41:50,800
We will take on anything, any combination of the latest aircraft that we can throw at ourselves, and we usually win.

491
00:41:53,660 --> 00:41:58,740
To date, 27 F-822 Raptors have been delivered to the U.S. Air Force

492
00:41:58,741 --> 00:42:03,860
and are in limited operation at Edwards, Nellis, and Tyndall Air Force bases.

493
00:42:06,020 --> 00:42:07,400
Many more will follow.

494
00:42:09,260 --> 00:42:14,440
The Air Force has agreed to a final production run of almost 300 aircraft.

495
00:42:14,441 --> 00:42:20,480
I think 300 F-822s will tip the scales of any conflict in our favor.

496
00:42:21,180 --> 00:42:27,800
I think that any country who sees 300 F-822s flying towards it has got to get a little nervous.

497
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:34,000
Lockheed expects a full production rate of 60 aircraft a year.

498
00:42:34,001 --> 00:42:41,640
And with components and parts coming from 46 states, the F-A-22 is truly a national effort.

499
00:42:48,240 --> 00:42:54,720
Costing a massive $93 million each, the Raptor is certainly the world's most expensive fighter aircraft.

500
00:42:55,360 --> 00:42:58,080
But for many, it is money well spent.

501
00:42:58,081 --> 00:43:00,223
Yeah, this is something special.

502
00:43:00,224 --> 00:43:04,240
This is not like an F-15 on steroids or an F-16 on steroids.

503
00:43:04,560 --> 00:43:09,760
This is the real thing with a leap of technology that's orders of magnitude better than what we have now.

504
00:43:12,040 --> 00:43:15,900
The interesting thing about the F-22, though, is it is a fixed volume

505
00:43:15,901 --> 00:43:22,060
with an infinitely increasing ability in terms of its computer capabilities.

506
00:43:22,061 --> 00:43:27,860
If you think about the airplane, it has fixed holes in the sides of the fuselage where computers sit today.

507
00:43:28,080 --> 00:43:34,880
In the future, there will be more computer capacity that requires less power going in that same physical hole.

508
00:43:34,881 --> 00:43:43,220
So the F-822, over the course of its life, will become a more and more flexible and more and more potent machine as computer capacity increases.

509
00:43:47,240 --> 00:43:55,080
America's F-822 Raptor was created out of the Cold War fear that Russian-made fighters would sweep aside the F-15.

510
00:43:58,080 --> 00:44:02,620
But the world has changed since the frigid days of the Cold War.

511
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:09,440
The Soviet Union no longer exists.

512
00:44:10,640 --> 00:44:17,181
And the F-15 has more than earned the fear and respect it commands as the current front-line fighter for the U.S.

513
00:44:17,182 --> 00:44:17,882
Air Force.

514
00:44:17,883 --> 00:44:28,920
but the raptor lockheed's fa-22 looks set to carry u.s air force doctrine well into the 21st century

515
00:44:28,921 --> 00:44:38,660
i'd be terrified to to go into the arena with something like that i really would

516
00:44:38,661 --> 00:44:44,980
i've been on the receiving end of it in testing and and mentally translated myself to what if

517
00:44:44,981 --> 00:44:49,940
This was combat, and it is an unnerving and a disquieting feeling

518
00:44:49,941 --> 00:44:52,520
to fly in the same airspace with one of these airplanes.

519
00:44:53,120 --> 00:44:55,780
I would say that it's not fair to our enemies

520
00:44:55,781 --> 00:44:58,260
or even our own technology to fight against the Raptor,

521
00:44:58,400 --> 00:45:01,360
but the goal of war isn't fair, that we don't go into combat

522
00:45:01,361 --> 00:45:03,480
because we want a fair fight.

523
00:45:03,680 --> 00:45:07,860
We want to win as fast as possible with as little loss of life as possible,

524
00:45:08,160 --> 00:45:10,700
and the Raptor allows that to do that for generations to come.

525
00:45:11,760 --> 00:45:14,940
In today's changing world, there are few certainties.

526
00:45:14,980 --> 00:45:21,980
But the rule of the Raptor, America's air-dominance fighter over the skies, is one of them.

527
00:45:29,980 --> 00:45:31,980
It's lopsided battles in naval history.

528
00:45:31,981 --> 00:45:34,980
On one side, the Yamato, the world's biggest battleship.

529
00:45:34,981 --> 00:45:40,980
On the other side, America's Taffy 3, um, not the world's biggest battleship.

530
00:45:40,981 --> 00:45:43,980
But don't place your bets yet, because the outcome is quite unexpected.

531
00:45:44,980 --> 00:45:49,180
Death of the Japanese Navy on Dogfights, next on the History Channel.

